Let $\lambda,h>0$ and suppose that $$|r|,\ |s|,\ |\lambda r|,\ |s/\lambda|\le h.$$ The four shears, in chronological order, $$\label{eq:shears}
 \mathsf Y(-\lambda),\quad \mathsf X(\lambda^{-1}-1),\quad
 \mathsf Y(1),\quad \mathsf X(\lambda-1)$$ send $(r,s)$ to $(\lambda r,s/\lambda)$. At every intermediate endpoint, and throughout every partially completed shear, both coordinates have absolute value at most $2h$.

Let $g$ be a smooth mean-zero vector field on $[0,\infty)\times\mathbb T^3$, effectively evaluable together with all mixed derivatives and with effective bounds for those derivatives on every finite time interval. There is a unique smooth mean-zero $\phi$ satisfying $\Delta\phi=\mathop{\mathrm{div}}g$. Both $\phi$ and $g_{\rm sol}=g-\nabla\phi$ are effective, and $g_{\rm sol}$ is divergence free and mean zero. For every $h\geq0$ and spatial multi-index $\mu$, $$\label{eq:projection-estimate}
 \|\partial_t^h\partial_X^\mu\nabla\phi(t)\|_\infty
 \leq C_\mu\max_{1\leq i\leq3}
 \|\partial_t^h\partial_{X_i}^{|\mu|+5}g(t)\|_\infty.$$ Thus, when all mixed derivatives of $g$ are bounded, square-integrable in time in spatial supremum norm, or rapidly decaying, the same property holds for $g_{\rm sol}$. Here rapid decay means a finite supremum after multiplication by $(1+t)^A$, for every $A\geq0$ and every fixed derivative order. Time periodicity and stationarity after a given time are also preserved. If $(U,P)$ solves the equation with force $g$, then it solves it with force $g_{\rm sol}$ and pressure $P-\phi$. More precisely, any common pointwise time weight on the finitely many derivatives on the right of <a href="#eq:projection-estimate" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:projection-estimate">[eq:projection-estimate]</a> passes to the derivative on its left. Thus a specified polynomial bound is preserved whenever those higher spatial derivatives have that bound.
